    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Overview and Motivation\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute capacity of a single accelerator. Two complementary parallelism strategies address this during inference:\n\n- **Tensor parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of every layer and processes the *same* token sequence simultaneously.\n- **Expert parallelism (EP)**: Splits the *experts* of a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layer across devices. Each device hosts a subset of experts; different tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, large MoE models (e.g., DeepSeek-V3, Mixtral-class architectures, Switch-style transformers) combine both: dense/attention layers use TP, MoE layers use EP, and both are often combined with data parallelism (DP) across replicated model copies. The design goal is to maximize aggregate memory bandwidth and FLOPs utilization while keeping communication overhead below the compute it enables.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 Sharding scheme\n\nConsider a transformer block with attention and an MLP. For a TP group of size `T`:\n\n**Attention:**\n- Query, Key, Value projection weights `W_Q, W_K, W_V \u2208 R^{d_model \u00d7 (3\u00b7d_model)}` are column-parallel: each rank holds `d_model \u00d7 (3\u00b7d_model/T)`.\n- Output projection `W_O` is row-parallel: each rank holds `(d_model/T) \u00d7 d_model`.\n- Head boundaries align with shard boundaries \u2014 `d_model/T` must be divisible by head size so no head is split across ranks.\n\n**MLP:**\n- First projection `W_up/W_gate` (columns): each rank holds `d_model \u00d7 (2\u00b7d_ff/T)` (for gated variants like SwiGLU,",
